    <h1>White-necked Raven Nesting: Breeding and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven (<em>Corvus albicollis</em>) nests on cliff faces and rocky outcrops across the mountains of eastern and southern Africa. The nest is a large stick structure on a ledge or in a cliff crevice. Clutch size is typically 3–5 eggs. Breeding season varies by location: July–October in East Africa; July–November in South Africa.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven is primarily a cliff-nester throughout its range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary sites:</strong> ledges and crevices on steep cliff faces in highland gorges, escarpments, and rocky mountains — the Rift Valley escarpment, Drakensberg, Ethiopian highlands, and East African uplands.</li>
      <li><strong>Human structures:</strong> occasionally nests on large buildings and bridges in areas where natural cliff sites are limited.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pairs maintain strong site fidelity — returning to the same cliff face and often the same ledge in successive years, adding material to an existing nest. Well-used nests become large over time.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure and Eggs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large platform of sticks and branches, lined with softer materials including wool (from sheep in pastoral areas), hair, grass, and bark. Clutch: typically 3–5 eggs. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish-white with heavy brown and grey blotching. Incubation: approximately 20–21 days by the female with male provisioning. Chicks fledge at approximately 6 weeks; post-fledging dependence continues for several weeks.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding timing varies across the species' range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>East Africa (Kenya, Tanzania, Ethiopia):</strong> breeding documented July–October, coinciding with the inter-monsoon dry season.</li>
      <li><strong>Southern Africa (South Africa, Zimbabwe):</strong> breeding July–November — late dry season into early summer.</li>
      <li><strong>Generalisation:</strong> the species tends to breed in the drier part of its local annual cycle, consistent with the pattern of other large African corvids.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

